import json
MAD="MadCLIP: Few-shot Medical Anomaly Detection with CLIP"; RECAP="Beyond Static Anchors: Bounded Prototype Conditioning for Language-Free Medical Anomaly Detection"
MLCA="Fine-Tuning CLIP Models with Convolutional Features for Medical Anomaly Detection"
SFAD="Bridging Vision Foundation Model Priors with CLIP for Spatial-aware Few-shot Anomaly Detection in Medical Images"
GAN="G-Anomaly: A Pyramid Graph Transformer-Based Vision-Language Model for General Industrial Anomaly Detection"
PA=("Leave-one-modality-out zero-shot training on BMAD (RESC and OCT17 held out together); pixel metrics on held-out BrainMRI, LiverCT, RESC; checkpoints and thresholds selected on source validation only; "
    "mask-supervised MVFA re-run under the same rule; 3 seeds, mean and std. ")
PB=("Leave-one-modality-out on BMAD (RESC and OCT17 held out together), MVFA retrained with source-validation selection; K in {2,4,8,16}; 3 seeds x 3 support draws. Baselines: transductive MVFA pipeline, support z-score with max pooling. ")
O={}
def off(tag,t,s,h,r,m,e,p):
    O[tag]="```json\n"+json.dumps({"Title":t,"Summary":s,"Hypothesis":h,"Rationale":r,"Method":m,"Experiment":e,"Provenance":p},indent=1,ensure_ascii=False)+"\n```"

off("stage3/A1/g1/o2/substitution",
 "Joint-bilateral upsampling of patch pseudo-masks guided by the raw image",
 "Replace superpixel majority voting with a guided (joint-bilateral) filter that upsamples the 17x17 patch pseudo-mask score to 240 px using the raw image as the edge guide, then threshold.",
 "Guided-filter upsampling of the continuous patch pseudo-mask score raises pseudo-mask Dice against true masks by at least 8 points over nearest-neighbour upsampled patch pseudo-masks, at least as much as SLIC snapping, and MVFA trained on the guided masks reaches at least 90% of the mask-supervised pixel AUROC on held-out masked modalities.",
 "The parent's goal, putting pseudo-mask boundaries on image edges, is kept; the technique changes. SLIC majority voting makes a hard decision per superpixel and depends on n_segments, which interacts with lesion size and fails when a superpixel straddles the boundary. Joint-bilateral upsampling (the guided filter of He et al.) is the standard technique for upsampling a low-resolution map under a high-resolution guide: it transfers the guide's edges to the map while preserving the map's values, works on continuous scores (so the threshold is applied after refinement), and has two interpretable parameters (radius, epsilon). It therefore addresses the same verified cause, the 14-pixel patch grid at 240 px, with fewer discrete artefacts.",
 "1) make_pseudo_masks.py: continuous score per patch u_p = (d_p - t_95)/(t_99 - t_95) clipped to [0, 1] (label-restricted rule, source-validation percentiles). 2) Bilinear upsampling of u to 240 px. 3) cv2.ximgproc.guidedFilter(guide = normalised raw image, src = upsampled u, radius = 8, eps = 1e-3) (or a 20-line PyTorch box-filter implementation). 4) Mask = refined u > 0.5 on abnormal images; normal images empty. 5) dataset/medical_zero.py loads masks; train_zero.py unchanged (Focal + Dice). 6) radius in {4, 8, 16} and eps in {1e-4, 1e-3, 1e-2} selected on source validation where source masks exist. MVFA hyper-parameters otherwise.",
 PA+"Conditions: true masks; nearest-neighbour patch pseudo-masks; SLIC-snapped (parent); guided-filter refined. Masks removed from 1 and 3 masked sources. Metrics: pseudo-mask Dice, boundary F1, trained pixel AUROC, Dice, image AUROC. Falsified if the guided filter does not raise pseudo-mask Dice by 8 points over nearest-neighbour masks or is worse than SLIC snapping.",
 [MLCA])

off("stage3/A1/g1/o6/out_of_box",
 "Detection-branch veto: MVFA's image-label-trained detection maps filter pseudo-masks for the segmentation branch",
 "Use MVFA's own detection branch, which is trained with reliable image-level labels on all six source datasets, to keep only those pseudo-mask patches that the detection branch also considers anomalous, and train the segmentation branch on the agreed patches.",
 "Restricting segmentation-branch targets to patches where the label-restricted pseudo-mask and the top-20% patches of the detection branch's map agree raises pseudo-mask precision by at least 15 points at a recall loss below 10 points, and MVFA trained this way recovers at least 90% of the mask-supervised pixel AUROC on held-out masked modalities.",
 "The inspiring idea trains only on confident pseudo-labels. By analogy with co-training and co-teaching, where two views of the data filter each other's noisy labels, MVFA already contains a second view: the detection adapters are trained with an image-level BCE on mean-pooled patch maps for every source image (verified in train_zero.py), so their patch maps learn where image-level evidence comes from, using reliable labels and all six datasets. Agreement between this weakly supervised view and the feature-distance pseudo-masks is a confidence signal internal to the target method, needing no external model; disagreeing patches are ignored rather than relabelled. This uses MVFA's two-branch architecture as a self-checking mechanism, which none of the cited works do.",
 "1) Stage 1: train MVFA's det branch only (train_zero.py with seg loss disabled) on all six sources for 20 epochs, selected on source validation image AUROC. 2) Detection patch map D(p) per abnormal source training image (level-averaged, as MVFA computes it). 3) Label-restricted pseudo-mask M(p) from frozen CLIP features. 4) Targets: positive where M(p) = 1 and D(p) in the image's top 20%; negative where M(p) = 0 and D(p) below the image median; ignore otherwise; normal images all negative. 5) Stage 2: train both branches as in MVFA (det branch continues from stage 1), seg loss = masked Focal + Dice over non-ignored pixels. 6) Agreement quantile (top 10/20/30%) selected on source validation. Hyper-parameters as MVFA.",
 PA+"Conditions: true masks; pseudo-masks alone; detection map alone as targets; agreement targets. Masks removed from all 3 masked sources, and the image-only datasets added as pseudo-mask sources. Metrics: pseudo-mask precision/recall vs true masks, trained pixel AUROC, Dice, image AUROC. Falsified if agreement does not raise precision by 15 points or the trained model does not outperform both single-view targets.",
 [MAD, RECAP])

off("stage3/A1/g1/o4/out_of_box",
 "Context-prediction residual pseudo-masks: a normal-trained masked-feature predictor localises lesions",
 "Train a small transformer on normal source images to predict a masked patch's CLIP feature from its surrounding patches, and use the prediction residual on abnormal images as a context-aware pseudo-mask for MVFA's segmentation branch.",
 "Pseudo-masks from the context-prediction residual reach at least 10 points higher Dice against true masks than nearest-neighbour pseudo-masks on BrainMRI and LiverCT, where anatomy varies with position, and MVFA trained on them recovers at least 90% of the mask-supervised pixel AUROC on held-out masked modalities.",
 "The inspiring idea spreads evidence over a structural affinity graph. A different analogy comes from inpainting and from radiologists' reading: a region is abnormal when it is not what its surroundings predict. Nearest-neighbour pseudo-masks ignore context, so a normal structure that is rare in the bank (e.g. ventricles at an unusual slice) is flagged, while a lesion that resembles normal tissue elsewhere is missed. A model that predicts each patch's feature from its context, trained only on normal images, captures anatomy-conditional normality; its residual is a context-aware anomaly score. Training is cheap because it operates on frozen 17x17 CLIP feature grids, not pixels.",
 "1) Extract frozen CLIP layer-24 patch features (17x17x1024) for all normal source training images. 2) Predictor: 4-layer transformer (width 256, 8 heads) with learned 2-D position embeddings; input = feature grid with 15% of patches replaced by a mask token (block masking, 3x3 blocks); output = predicted features at masked positions; loss = 1 - cosine. Train 30 epochs, AdamW lr 3e-4, batch 64 (minutes on one GPU). 3) Residual r_p = 1 - cos(f_p, f_hat_p) with each patch masked once (sliding 3x3 block masking, averaged). 4) Threshold at the 99th percentile of residuals on normal source-validation images; label-restricted to abnormal images. 5) dataset/medical_zero.py loads masks; train_zero.py unchanged. Hyper-parameters as stated; MVFA training as in the paper.",
 PA+"Conditions: true masks; NN pseudo-masks; context-residual pseudo-masks; their union and intersection. Masks removed from all 3 masked sources. Metrics: pseudo-mask Dice per dataset, trained pixel AUROC, Dice, image AUROC. Falsified if residual pseudo-masks do not beat NN pseudo-masks by 10 Dice points on BrainMRI or LiverCT.",
 [SFAD])

off("stage3/B1/g1/o5/out_of_box",
 "Upper-tail Wasserstein score: comparing each image's patch-score distribution with the support-normal distribution",
 "Score each test image by the 1-Wasserstein distance between the upper tail of its own patch-score distribution and the upper tail of the pooled leave-one-out support-normal patch scores, giving a per-image score that needs no test-set statistics and is insensitive to single pixels.",
 "The upper-tail (top 10%) Wasserstein score achieves image AUROC within 1 point of the transductive pipeline on all held-out modalities, at least 1 point above max-pixel scoring on modalities with diffuse abnormalities (RESC, OCT17, ChestXray), and loses less than 0.5 AUROC points when 3 spurious spikes are injected per normal test map.",
 "The inspiring idea aggregates excess evidence over a spatial region. By analogy with distribution-shift detection, an abnormal image can be viewed as one whose distribution of patch scores is shifted upward in its tail relative to normal anatomy, whether the shift is concentrated (focal lesion) or diffuse (fluid, opacities). The Wasserstein distance between upper-tail quantile functions measures this shift in score units, is defined from the support normals alone, and changes by at most 1/n per spurious pixel. Unlike max or region mass, it needs no spatial connectivity assumption, which matters for diffuse findings in OCT and chest X-ray where connected regions are ill-defined.",
 "1) Fused patch map m (support LOO z-scores per branch and level, averaged; no min-max). 2) Reference: pooled LOO support patch scores; its upper-tail quantile function Q_ref(u) for u in [0.9, 1], evaluated on 50 points. 3) Test image: Q_img(u) over its 289 patches. 4) Score = mean over u of max(Q_img(u) - Q_ref(u), 0) (one-sided W1 on the tail). 5) Tail fraction (5, 10, 20%) selected on source episodes. 6) test_few.py: implement; remove min-max. No training.",
 PB+"Ordered ablation: (i) support z-score with max pooling, (ii) top-k mean, (iii) upper-tail Wasserstein. Metrics: image AUROC per modality, sensitivity at 95% specificity (threshold from LOO support scores), AUROC under spike injection, score shift under resampling. Falsified if AUROC drops more than 1 point or it does not beat max pooling on at least two diffuse-abnormality modalities.",
 [GAN, MAD])
json.dump(O, open("logs/answers/run2_qualsg/s3_g1_mut.json","w"), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False); print(len(O))
